          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have asthma and I use a blue inhaler.",
          "answer": "The guidance I found doesn't say what a blue inhaler is for. Please check with your clinician or pharmacist which inhaler you should use every day and which one is for sudden symptoms.\n\nThe sources do explain why the difference matters. The asthma overview (*Asthma*, condition summary) describes a daily anti-inflammatory inhaler, a reliever for symptoms, and a written action plan for when symptoms get worse. The guideline note (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach*) says treatment is reviewed regularly and that inhaler technique and use are checked before changing treatment. The adherence summary (*Sticking with asthma treatment*) suggests a spacer can improve how medicine reaches the lungs, and that having a clinician watch you use your inhaler is more useful than a leaflet.\n\n**Get emergency help straight away** if you have breathlessness at rest, can't speak in full sentences, have blue or gray lips or fingertips, feel drowsy or confused, or if your reliever doesn't help or symptoms come back within a few hours. The emergency guidance (*Severe difficulty breathing*) lists these signs.\n\nIf you'd like, I can explain any of these topics in more detail, such as what an action plan usually includes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
