          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Should I be on something else as well?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you whether you should be on anything else. That decision depends on your own symptoms and history, and only your clinician can make it.\n\nThe asthma guideline note (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) describes treatment in steps. Your clinician chooses the step based on how well your asthma is controlled and whether you've had flare-ups. The note says the step is \"reviewed rather than fixed,\" so the plan can go up or down over time. Before a clinician steps treatment up, they check your inhaler technique and whether you're using your medicine as prescribed. The note says these two things account for a large share of apparent treatment failure.\n\nAsk your clinician or pharmacist:\n- Is my current treatment the right step for how my asthma is controlled?\n- Do I need a daily preventer inhaler in addition to my reliever?\n- Could you check how I use my inhaler?\n\n**Get emergency help straight away** if you have breathlessness at rest, can't speak in full sentences, have blue or gray lips or fingertips, feel drowsy or confused, or if your reliever doesn't help or symptoms come back within a few hours.\n\nThe search results did not cover which specific medicines suit any particular person, or doses. Those are outside this tool's scope.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
